diff --git a/mailnews/protocols/jmap/docs/architecture.md b/mailnews/protocols/jmap/docs/architecture.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000000..8c57eca8d09
--- /dev/null
+++ b/mailnews/protocols/jmap/docs/architecture.md
@@ -0,0 +1,522 @@
+# JMAP support in Thunderbird: architecture and implementation plan
+
+Status: draft, updated 2026-11-08.
+
+Progress:
+
+- **M0 (protocol client): implemented.** RFC 8620/8621 client, transport,
+  capabilities, errors, blob handling and xpcshell tests live in
+  `mailnews/protocols/jmap/`.
+- **M1 (account scaffolding): implemented, not yet compiled.** `IJmapClient`
+  and the JS XPCOM implementation, `IJmapIncomingServer`, `JmapIncomingServer`,
+  `JmapFolder`, `JmapProtocolInfo` and component registrations are in place.
+  The C++ has never been through a compiler in this workspace (which has no
+  `mach`/Firefox tree), so building and fixing it is the first task on a
+  machine with a full Thunderbird build.
+- **M2 (metadata sync): implemented, not yet compiled.** The JS sync engine,
+  the folder/database adapter and the tests are in place; pushing and body
+  display come later.
+- **M3 (message display): implemented, not yet compiled.** Raw message blobs
+  and a pure-JS RFC 5322 reconstruction fallback are in place; the C++ message
+  service, channel and protocol handler mirror Exchange.
+- **M4 (mutations and submission): mostly implemented, not yet compiled.**
+  Read, flagged, junk, delete, move and copy are translated into JMAP calls
+  from the folder; `copyFileMessage` (drafts/Sent/templates) and
+  `EmailSubmission/set` are still missing.
+- **M5 and later: not started.**
+
+This document describes how the JSON Meta Application Protocol (JMAP) is being
+added to Thunderbird. It explains the constraints imposed by the existing mail
+architecture, proposes a layered design, and breaks the work into milestones
+that can land and be tested independently.
+
+JMAP consists of two specifications:
+
+- [RFC 8620](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8620) defines the core protocol:
+  session discovery, request/response envelopes, method calls, error handling,
+  blobs, push notifications and query/changes semantics.
+- [RFC 8621](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8621) defines the mail data
+  model: `Mailbox`, `Email`, `Thread`, `Identity`, `EmailSubmission`,
+  `VacationResponse` and `SearchSnippet`.
+
+## Goals
+
+- Add `jmap` as a first-class account type: create, configure, sync, read,
+  search, modify and send mail against any RFC 8620/8621 server.
+- Map JMAP state changes onto Thunderbird's existing folder/message database
+  model instead of inventing a second storage stack.
+- Keep the protocol layer independently testable, without a running server
+  binary and without a C++/Rust round-trip for every operation.
+- Share authentication, account setup and UI code with IMAP/EWS/Graph.
+- Support incremental sync (`/changes`, state tokens) and server push
+  (EventSource or WebSocket) from the start of the sync work, not as an
+  afterthought.
+
+## Non-goals (for the first usable version)
+
+- JMAP Contacts and Calendars. The capability URIs are recognised, but the
+  address book and calendar providers are separate follow-up projects.
+- JMAP Sieve, Quota and MDN extensions.
+- Full offline mode for the first milestone. `nsIMsgDatabase` already gives
+  us an offline metadata cache; queued offline mutations come later.
+
+## How Thunderbird models a protocol today
+
+A mail account type is a set of classes looked up by the type string
+(`imap`, `pop3`, `ews`, `graph`, ...):
+
+| Purpose | Contract ID | Base class |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Incoming server | `@mozilla.org/messenger/server;1?type=jmap` | `nsMsgIncomingServer` |
+| Folder | `@mozilla.org/mail/folder-factory;1?name=jmap` | `nsMsgDBFolder` |
+| Protocol info | `@mozilla.org/messenger/protocol/info;1?type=jmap` | `MsgProtocolInfo` |
+| Message service | `@mozilla.org/messenger/messageservice;1?type=jmap` | `nsIMsgMessageService` |
+| Outgoing server | `@mozilla.org/messenger/outgoing/server;1?type=jmap` | `nsIMsgOutgoingServer` |
+
+The server owns per-account preferences (`hostname`, `username`, `type`,
+`jmap_url`, ...) and produces folder objects. A folder owns a Mork `.msf`
+database (metadata) and may own a message store (`mbox`/maildir) for downloaded
+RFC 5322 messages. The UI, filtering, search, threading, offline support and
+message display all sit on top of `nsIMsgDBHdr` and `nsIMsgFolder`.
+
+Two facts from `mailnews/base/docs/folder_storage.md` drive the design:
+
+1. The local database and message display are RFC 5322-centric. JMAP delivers
+   structured JSON bodies, so they must be converted into RFC 5322 for
+   display and for storing downloaded copies.
+2. The message database is per folder. JMAP has a many-to-many relationship
+   between `Email` and `Mailbox`, so a single server email may need a local
+   header in more than one folder.
+
+Existing HTTP-based protocols (EWS, Graph) implement the account/folder
+classes in C++ and the wire client in Rust, connected over XPCOM. That split
+is valuable for protocols with complex XML/SOAP stacks and shared client
+infrastructure. JMAP is JSON over HTTPS with a small, uniform method interface,
+so the wire layer is implemented in JavaScript first (see "Language choice").
+
+## Proposed architecture
+
+Five layers, each with a narrow boundary:
+
+```
++--------------------------------------------------------------+
+| UI, account setup, filters, search, message display          |
++--------------------------------------------------------------+
+| Account integration: IncomingServer, Folder, ProtocolInfo,   |
+| MessageService, OutgoingServer (C++, mirrors EWS/Graph)      |
++--------------------------------------------------------------+
+| Sync engine (JS): state tokens, /changes loops, threading,   |
+| flag/keyword reconciliation, queued mutations                |
++--------------------------------------------------------------+
+| JMAP client (JS): session, request batching, Mailbox/Email/  |
+| Thread/Identity/EmailSubmission, blob upload/download, push  |
++--------------------------------------------------------------+
+| Transport (JS): fetch, OAuth2/Basic auth, retries, timeouts, |
+| proxy/cookie policy inherited from Necko                     |
++--------------------------------------------------------------+
+```
+
+### Layer 1: transport and authentication
+
+`JmapTransport` wraps `fetch` and provides:
+
+- asynchronous auth header injection, so OAuth2 access tokens can be
+  refreshed between requests (the existing `OAuth2.sys.mjs` module is the
+  token source);
+- Basic authentication as a fallback, built with the existing login manager;
+- request timeouts and cancellation;
+- retry with `Retry-After` for `429`, `502`, `503` and `504` (GET only by
+  default, because retrying a state-changing POST is not safe);
+- uniform error types for HTTP problems (`application/problem+json`),
+  authentication failures, rate limits and network failures.
+
+No new HTTP stack is introduced: traffic goes through the same Necko
+implementation as every other Thunderbird protocol, so proxy settings,
+certificate handling and developer-tool inspection keep working.
+
+### Layer 2: JMAP client
+
+`JmapClient` implements RFC 8620:
+
+- session discovery at `/.well-known/jmap` and session validation;
+- capability negotiation and per-account capability lookup;
+- request envelope construction (`using`, `methodCalls`, `createdIds`),
+  automatic capability derivation from method names, request size and call
+  count limits;
+- method-level error separation (`JmapMethodError`) from request-level
+  errors (`JmapRequestError`);
+- `sessionState` tracking and change notification;
+- upload/download URL templates and size limits;
+- a concurrency limiter driven by `maxConcurrentRequests`.
+
+`JmapMailClient` implements RFC 8621 on top of it: mailbox tree, email query
+and fetch (including body values), threads, identities, submissions, vacation
+response, `SearchSnippet`, and typed wrappers for the `/changes`, `/query`,
+`/queryChanges` and `/set` families.
+
+### Layer 3: account integration
+
+New C++ classes, modelled on the Exchange implementation:
+
+- `JmapIncomingServer : nsMsgIncomingServer`
+  - owns the account preferences and a `JmapClient` instance;
+  - implements `VerifyLogon`, `GetNewMessages`, `Shutdown`, `GetCanCreateFolders`,
+    `GetDefaultCopiesAndFolders`, biff/`nsIMsgBiffManager` behaviour.
+- `JmapFolder : nsMsgDBFolder`
+  - represents one `Mailbox` and owns its `.msf` database;
+  - implements `UpdateFolder`, `GetMessages`, `FetchMessage`,
+    `MarkMessagesRead`/`Deleted`, `DeleteMessages`, `MoveCopyMessages`;
+  - maps `Mailbox.role` to Thunderbird special folders
+    (`inbox`, `archive`, `drafts`, `sent`, `trash`, `junk`, `all`, `flagged`,
+    `important`).
+- `JmapProtocolInfo` (derived from `MsgProtocolInfo`) for the default local
+  path and account wizard capabilities.
+- `JmapService : nsIMsgMessageService` for `jmap-message://` URIs, so message
+  display and attachments work like every other protocol.
+- `JmapOutgoingServer : nsIMsgOutgoingServer` implementing `EmailSubmission`
+  and mapping `nsIMsgCompFields` to a JMAP `Email` + envelope.
+
+The C++ classes talk to the JS client through a small XPCOM interface
+(`IJmapClient`) rather than importing modules directly from C++; this keeps
+the same clean boundary that Exchange has with its Rust crates and lets the
+implementation move to Rust later without touching folder/server code.
+
+### Layer 4: sync engine
+
+A JS sync component owns the state machine for one account:
+
+- initial sync: `Mailbox/get` builds the folder tree; `Email/query` +
+  `Email/get` (metadata properties only) populates the database;
+- incremental sync: `Mailbox/changes`, `Email/changes`, `Thread/changes`,
+  `Identity/changes`, with `newState` persisted per data type in the account
+  preferences or a small SQLite state file;
+- `cannotCalculateChanges` and `stateMismatch` trigger a bounded full
+  resync of that data type;
+- email ids are strings, while Thunderbird message keys are `uint32_t`.
+  Every header stores the JMAP id in a string property (`jmapId`), and each
+  folder maintains a `jmapId -> nsMsgKey` index. New headers get local keys
+  from the folder's key sequence. Because JMAP allows one email in several
+  mailboxes, sync creates one local header per mailbox membership and keeps
+  `jmapId` shared; moves/copies are reconciled against the authoritative
+  `mailboxIds` set rather than assumed to be one-to-one.
+
+### Layer 5: message body conversion and push
+
+Display requires RFC 5322. `JmapMessageBuilder` will:
+
+- fetch the `Email` with `bodyValues`, `textBody`, `htmlBody` and
+  `attachments`;
+- reconstruct `Subject`, `From`, `To`, `Cc`, `Bcc`, `Reply-To`, `Date`,
+  `Message-ID`, `In-Reply-To`, `References` from the JMAP metadata;
+- convert `textBody`/`htmlBody` parts into MIME entities, preserving
+  `charset`, `Content-Type`, `Content-Transfer-Encoding` and `cid` references;
+- stream attachments from `downloadUrl` on demand, so large messages are not
+  downloaded until needed.
+
+Push uses `eventSourceUrl` (RFC 8620 §7.3) with a streaming `nsIHttpChannel`
+consumer on the main thread, falling back to polling with a backoff based on
+the server's push capability. WebSocket push (`urn:ietf:params:jmap:websocket`)
+can be added behind the same interface later.
+
+## Language choice
+
+The client and sync layers are JavaScript, and the account/folder scaffolding
+is C++.
+
+Reasons:
+
+- JMAP's entities are JSON and its control flow is asynchronous; JavaScript
+  expresses that directly and the whole client can be exercised in xpcshell
+  against a mock transport, with no native build.
+- Thunderbird already implements OAuth2, login management, account discovery
+  and much of the UI in JS. The account wizard can validate a JMAP session
+  with the same code the account will use later.
+- The protocol client is small enough (roughly the size of
+  `mailnews/base/src/OAuth2.sys.mjs` plus a few thousand lines) that a JS
+  implementation is not a performance concern; request volume is per-minute,
+  not per-message-view, and bodies are handled by Necko streams.
+- Exchange needed Rust because of SOAP/XML complexity and shared HTTP
+  infrastructure. JMAP does not.
+
+If profiling later shows a bottleneck in the wire layer, `JmapClient` can be
+reimplemented as a Rust XPCOM client behind the same `IJmapClient` interface
+without changing folder/server/sync code. The JS implementation would remain
+as the account-setup and conformance-test implementation.
+
+## Data mapping
+
+| JMAP | Thunderbird |
+| --- | --- |
+| `Mailbox.id` | `nsIMsgFolder` URI (`jmap://user@host/encoded-id`), folder name from `Mailbox.name` |
+| `Mailbox.role` | special folder flags (`inbox`, `drafts`, `sent`, `trash`, `junk`, ...) |
+| `Mailbox.parentId` | folder hierarchy (`parent` relationship by URI) |
+| `Email.id` | `jmapId` string property on `nsIMsgDBHdr`, plus per-folder key index |
+| `Email.threadId` | thread id; `references`/`inReplyTo` still stored for display |
+| `Email.keywords` | `$seen` -> read, `$flagged` -> flagged, `$answered` -> replied, `$forwarded` -> forwarded, `$draft` -> draft, `$junk` -> junk; other keywords -> tags |
+| `Email.mailboxIds` | one local header per mailbox membership |
+| `Email.sentAt`/`receivedAt` | `date` header field; received time kept in `nsIMsgDBHdr` |
+| `Email.size` | `messageSize` |
+| `Email.preview` | `preview`/`teaser` |
+| `Email.hasAttachment` | attachment flag |
+| `Email.bodyValues`, `textBody`, `htmlBody`, `attachments` | RFC 5322 reconstruction (`JmapMessageBuilder`) |
+| `Identity` | `nsIMsgIdentity` |
+| `EmailSubmission` | outgoing server / `nsIMsgSend` result |
+| `Thread.emailIds` | thread membership, reconciled with message headers |
+| `state` strings | per-folder and per-account sync tokens |
+
+## Milestones
+
+### M0 - protocol client and conformance tests (this change)
+
+Delivered:
+
+- `mailnews/protocols/jmap/modules/JmapConstants.sys.mjs`
+- `mailnews/protocols/jmap/modules/JmapErrors.sys.mjs`
+- `mailnews/protocols/jmap/modules/JmapTransport.sys.mjs`
+- `mailnews/protocols/jmap/modules/JmapClient.sys.mjs`
+- `mailnews/protocols/jmap/modules/JmapMailClient.sys.mjs`
+- xpcshell tests with a mock transport covering session discovery, request
+  envelope construction, error handling, capability negotiation, upload and
+  download, and the RFC 8621 method wrappers.
+
+Exit criteria: `mach xpcshell-test mailnews/protocols/jmap/test/unit` passes;
+the client can drive a scripted JMAP conversation end to end.
+
+### M1 - account type scaffolding
+
+- C++ `JmapIncomingServer`, `JmapFolder` and `JmapProtocolInfo` and their
+  `components.conf` registrations. (`JmapService`/`jmap-message://` belongs to
+  the message display milestone.)
+- `IJmapClient` XPCOM interface (modelled on `IExchangeClient`) with
+  asynchronous operation callbacks. The implementation is a JS XPCOM
+  component registered from `components.conf` via `esModule`/`constructor`;
+  it wraps the `JmapClient`/`JmapMailClient` modules, so the C++ code never
+  imports JS modules directly and the client can move to Rust later without
+  touching folder or server code.
+- Preferences: `mail.jmap.enabled`, `mail.jmap.loglevel`, per-account
+  `jmap_session_url`, auth method.
+- Manual account setup: JMAP session URL + credentials.
+- `mailnews/protocols/moz.build` wiring (the `jmap` directory is picked up
+  from there by `mailnews/moz.build`).
+- Tests: account registration, protocol info, mailbox conversion, role
+  mapping and parent-before-child ordering.
+
+Exit criteria: a JMAP account can be created and its folder tree appears in
+the folder pane (read-only; no messages yet).
+
+Implementation notes:
+
+- Folder flags are derived from the JMAP `role` in JavaScript and delivered as
+  `IJmapMailbox.folderFlags`, so the C++ side does not duplicate the role
+  mapping.
+- `Mailbox/get` is sorted by tree depth before folders are created, because
+  JMAP does not guarantee parent-before-child ordering.
+- Sibling mailboxes with identical names are given a local disambiguation
+  suffix (`Name (2)`); the JMAP id remains the stable identity.
+- Authentication uses OAuth2 bearer tokens when the account's auth method is
+  OAuth2, and HTTP Basic otherwise.
+- Deferred to later milestones: the account wizard UI for JMAP, remote folder
+  creation/rename/delete, and message synchronization. Accounts can already be
+  created programmatically with
+  `MailServices.accounts.createIncomingServer(user, host, "jmap")`.
+
+### M2 - initial metadata sync
+
+- `JmapSync` engine: `Mailbox/get`, `Email/query`, `Email/get` with metadata
+  properties, thread and keyword reconciliation into `nsIMsgDatabase`.
+- Local key allocation and `jmapId` index.
+- `Email/changes`, `Mailbox/changes` state persistence and full-resync
+  fallback paths.
+- Biff/new-mail notification from `Email/changes` or push.
+- Tests: initial sync, incremental sync, deletion, move, keyword change,
+  `cannotCalculateChanges` fallback.
+
+Implementation notes:
+
+- `modules/JmapSync.sys.mjs` is the engine. It works against the
+  `JmapSyncStore` interface, so all reconciliation logic is tested with an
+  in-memory store.
+- `modules/JmapFolderStore.sys.mjs` implements the store on top of folders
+  and message databases. It indexes `jmapId` headers at the start of each sync
+  and writes headers with `createNewHdr`/`attachHdr`. Flag changes go through
+  `nsIMsgDatabase.mark*` so views and listeners are notified; additions and
+  deletions go through `nsIMsgFolderNotificationService`.
+- `modules/JmapMessage.sys.mjs` maps JMAP keywords, addresses, dates and
+  the `messageId`/`references` headers. The JMAP thread id is stored as a
+  string property; Threading uses the RFC 5322 `references` chain so the
+  database builds threads itself.
+- Sync state (`Email/changes`) is account-wide and stored on the root folder
+  as `jmapEmailState`. A full sync only flags messages as `New` when they
+  arrived through `Email/changes`, never for the initial population of an
+  existing account.
+- Moves and multi-mailbox membership are handled by diffing `mailboxIds`
+  against the folders which currently hold a header for the message.
+- Mailbox removal (folder deletion) is not implemented yet; folders whose
+  mailbox disappeared on the server are left alone.
+- The mailbox index enumerates every header once per sync. This is fine for
+  moderate accounts and is the first thing to optimize when profiling.
+- Message mutations are not yet sent back to the server (M4), so local
+  read/flag changes are overwritten by the next sync. Likewise, opening a
+  message still fails: body reconstruction is M3.
+
+Status: implemented, not yet compiled against a full Thunderbird tree.
+Biff and new-mail notification rely on the `New` flag and `notifyMsgAdded`;
+push (`EventSource`) is not implemented.
+
+Exit criteria: message lists are correct and survive restart; new mail
+notifications work.
+
+### M3 - message display
+
+- `JmapMessageBuilder`: RFC 5322 reconstruction, MIME tree, attachments,
+  `cid:` rewriting.
+- `JmapService` / `jmap-message://` channel integration, save-as, print,
+  forwarding/quoting.
+- On-demand body fetch and blob download, with caching in the folder store.
+- Tests: text-only, HTML-only, multipart/alternative, attachments,
+  non-ASCII headers, broken/invalid body values.
+
+Implementation notes:
+
+- Primary path: `Email/get` for `blobId` and `JmapClient.download()` for the
+  raw RFC 5322 message. The bytes are written to the folder's message store on
+  first display (`JmapFetchMsgsToOffline`), so later displays are local.
+- Fallback path: `JmapMimeEncoder.sys.mjs` + `JmapMessageBuilder.sys.mjs`
+  reconstruct an RFC 5322 message from `bodyStructure`, `bodyValues`,
+  `textBody`, `htmlBody` and `attachments`. Text bodies become UTF-8 with
+  quoted-printable when needed, attachments are base64, inline images are put
+  in `multipart/related` with `Content-ID` and `cid:` links left intact for
+  libmime to resolve.
+- `JmapService` implements `nsIMsgMessageService`; `JmapProtocolHandler`
+  registers the internal `x-moz-jmap` scheme; `JmapMessageChannel` reports
+  `message/rfc822`, runs the MIME converter when displaying, and serves
+  `?part=` requests.
+- `JmapUrl` is registered in `nsNewMailnewsURI.cpp` for the `jmap`,
+  `jmap-message` and `x-moz-jmap` schemes, because Thunderbird's content
+  policy expects message URLs to implement `nsIMsgMessageUrl`.
+- Messages are cached in the folder's message store on first display
+  (`JmapFolder::GetSupportsOffline` is true). Saving an attachment from a
+  reconstructed message is currently limited to inline images; raw-blob
+  accounts have the complete message locally.
+- The builder never trusts server strings: header values, addresses, MIME
+  types, charsets, filenames and cids are sanitized, headers are folded and
+  non-ASCII header text uses RFC 2047 encoded-words.
+- Stalwart compatibility: session documents without `core.versions` are
+  accepted, method errors may carry `description` instead of `detail`,
+  `bodyValues: null` and missing multipart `partId` are tolerated, and blob
+  download URLs always get a `name` and an `accept` type.
+
+Status: implemented, not yet compiled against a full Thunderbird tree.
+
+Exit criteria: every message kind used in the test suite displays correctly.
+
+### M4 - mutations and submission
+
+- `Email/set` for flags, keyword tags, mailbox membership, destroy.
+- `Email/copy`, `Email/import` for drafts and cross-account operations.
+- Delete/undo, move/copy, mark read/unread, junk, archive.
+- `EmailSubmission/set` with envelope, `onSuccessUpdateEmail`,
+  `sendAt` (delayed send), identity selection, save-to-sent.
+- SMTP fallback for servers that do not advertise submission.
+- Tests: every mutation, undo, draft save and send path.
+
+Implementation notes:
+
+- `modules/JmapMutations.sys.mjs` translates local operations into
+  `Email/set` patches: `true` sets a keyword or mailbox membership, `null`
+  clears it (never `false`). Batches respect the server's `maxObjectsInSet`,
+  and one stale `ifInState` is retried without the state token.
+- Read and flagged changes are wired end to end: `JmapFolder` overrides
+  `markMessagesRead`, `markMessagesFlagged` and `markAllMessagesRead`, updates
+  the local database first, then calls `IJmapClient.setMessagesRead`/
+  `setMessagesFlagged`, which run `JmapMutator`.
+- Delete, move and copy are wired as remote-first operations:
+  - `deleteMessages` permanently destroys server messages (`Email/set
+    destroy`) when `deleteStorage` is set or the folder is Trash. A normal
+    delete moves the message into the Trash mailbox by changing `mailboxIds`,
+    and falls back to a permanent delete when no Trash mailbox exists.
+  - `copyMessages` sends a membership change for moves and `Email/copy` for
+    copies. Moves keep the email id; copies get new server ids.
+  - After the server accepts the mutation, the source headers are removed
+    locally and an account sync creates the destination headers, so local
+    state always follows the server. This avoids inventing local ids for
+    server copies.
+  - `allowUndo` is currently ignored: there is no JMAP undo transaction yet,
+    so Undo does not reverse a move or copy.
+- Junk state: `setJunkScoreForMessages` pushes `$junk`/`$notjunk` keywords for
+  scores of exactly 100/0 (user decisions) and leaves intermediate classifier
+  scores to the server.
+- Server failures are logged, not surfaced as a failed user action; the next
+  `Email/changes` sync reconciles local and server state. There is no
+  optimistic rollback.
+- `markAllMessagesRead` collects the unread messages before marking them, so
+  only messages that actually change are sent.
+- Still missing: `copyFileMessage` (upload + `Email/import`) for saving
+  drafts/Sent/templates and attachment replacement, undo transactions,
+  cross-account copies, folder creation for archive year/month, and
+  submission with `EmailSubmission/set`.
+
+Status: partially implemented, not yet compiled.
+
+Exit criteria: a user can fully manage and send mail on a JMAP account.
+
+### M5 - push, autodiscovery and polish
+
+- EventSource push with reconnect/backoff; polling fallback.
+- SRV `_jmap._tcp` and `/.well-known/jmap` autodiscovery in account setup.
+- OAuth2 provider entries for known JMAP providers.
+- Offline mutation queue and replay.
+- Telemetry (session errors, sync failures, push latency), localized errors.
+- Tests: push reconnect, discovery, OAuth2, offline replay.
+
+## Testing strategy
+
+- Unit tests (xpcshell) for the client, transport and sync engine, using an
+  injected mock transport. No network, deterministic retries.
+- A `JmapServer` mock under `mailnews/test/resources`, modelled on
+  `EwsServer.sys.mjs` and `GraphServer.sys.mjs`, for account-level tests. It
+  will speak real HTTP and support scripted `methodResponses`, upload and
+  download.
+- Browser tests for account setup, folder pane population, message display,
+  and compose/send.
+- A conformance suite of recorded RFC 8620/8621 examples (session document,
+  request/response envelopes, error cases). These catch regressions in wire
+  format even without a live server.
+
+## Security and privacy
+
+- JMAP requires HTTPS; plain HTTP is only allowed for loopback test servers.
+  The account wizard enforces this, mirroring `mailnews` HTTP protocol
+  policies.
+- Credentials are held by the login manager/OAuth2 module, never in account
+  preferences or URLs.
+- `Authorization` is attached only to the session's own origin, never to
+  arbitrary redirect targets; cross-origin redirects are rejected.
+- The client enforces `maxSizeRequest`, `maxSizeUpload`,
+  `maxCallsInRequest` and `maxObjectsInGet` from the session document, which
+  bounds memory use by a hostile server.
+- HTML bodies are sanitised by the existing message display pipeline; remote
+  content blocking, attachment quarantine and link handling are inherited
+  from the existing code paths.
+- Push connections are authenticated with the same credentials and are
+  closed on account shutdown.
+
+## Risks and open questions
+
+1. **C++/JS boundary.** Calling a JS client from C++ folder code needs a
+   stable XPCOM interface and careful main-thread handling. Prototype in M1
+   before committing to it; moving the client to Rust later is the fallback.
+2. **Many-to-many mailboxes.** Duplicated local headers per membership must
+   not confuse threading, search, or `nsIMsgDatabase` key allocation. ADR
+   needed before M2.
+3. **RFC 5322 reconstruction fidelity.** Some messages (especially signed or
+   encrypted ones) cannot be faithfully rebuilt from JSON body values. Decide
+   whether to fetch the raw blob (`blobId` with `Content-Type:
+   message/rfc822` when the server advertises it) instead, and fall back to
+   reconstruction only when raw download is unavailable.
+4. **Push transport.** EventSource is not implemented for Thunderbird's
+   mail process today; a streaming channel consumer must be written and
+   tested against reconnect and proxy behaviour.
+5. **Bugzilla component.** The tree needs a `Mailnews Core :: Networking:
+   JMAP` component (or similar) before `BUG_COMPONENT` can be set in
+   `moz.build` files.
